          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 7 Control of Gene Expression"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.226,
            0.507,
            0.938
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "AN OVERVIEW OF GENE CONTROL 397  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Different Cell Types of a Multicellular Organism Contain the Same DNA 397  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Different Cell Types Synthesize Different Sets of RNAs and Proteins 398  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Spectrum of mRNAs Present in a Cell Can Be Used to Accurately Identify the Cell Type 400  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "External Signals Can Cause a Cell to Change the Expression of Its Genes 400  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Gene Expression Can Be Regulated at Many of the Steps in the Pathway from DNA to RNA to Protein 401  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 402  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CONTROL OF TRANSCRIPTION BY SEQUENCE-SPECIFIC DNA-BINDING PROTEINS 402  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Sequence of Nucleotides in the DNA Double Helix Can Be Read by Proteins 402  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Regulators Contain Structural Motifs That Can Read DNA Sequences 403  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Dimerization of Transcription Regulators Increases Their Affinity and Specificity for DNA 406  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Transcription Regulators Bind Cooperatively to DNA 407  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Nucleosome Structure Promotes Cooperative Binding of Transcription Regulators 408  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA-Binding by Transcription Regulators Is Dynamic 409  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 410  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "TRANSCRIPTION REGULATORS SWITCH GENES ON AND OFF 410  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Tryptophan Repressor Switches Genes Off 410  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Repressors Turn Genes Off and Activators Turn Them On 411  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Both an Activator and a Repressor Control the Lac Operon 412  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Looping Can Occur During Bacterial Gene Regulation 412  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Complex Switches Control Gene Transcription in Eukaryotes 414  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Eukaryotic Gene Control Region Includes Many cis-Regulatory Sequences 414  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Transcription Regulators Work in Groups 415  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Activator Proteins Promote the Assembly of RNA Polymerase at the Start Point of Transcription 416  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Transcription Activators Direct the Modification of Local Chromatin Structure 417  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Transcription Activators Work by Releasing Paused RNA Polymerase 418  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Activators Work Synergistically 419  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Condensate Formation Likely Increases the Efficiency of Transcription Initiation 420  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Transcription Repressors Can Inhibit Transcription in Several Ways 420  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Insulator DNA Sequences Prevent Eukaryotic Transcription Regulators from Influencing Distant Genes 422  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 422  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "MOLECULAR GENETIC MECHANISMS THAT CREATE AND MAINTAIN SPECIALIZED CELL TYPES 423  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Complex Genetic Switches That Regulate Drosophila Development Are Built Up from Smaller Modules 423  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Drosophila Eve Gene Is Regulated by Combinatorial Controls 424  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Regulators Are Brought into Play by Extracellular Signals 426  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Combinatorial Gene Control Creates Many Different Cell Types 427  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Specialized Cell Types Can Be Experimentally Reprogrammed to Become Pluripotent Stem Cells 428  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Combinations of Master Transcription Regulators Specify Cell Types by Controlling the Expression of Many Genes 429  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Specialized Cells Must Rapidly Turn Some Genes On and Off 430  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Differentiated Cells Maintain Their Identity 431"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.526,
            0.095,
            0.924,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Circuits Allow the Cell to Carry Out Logic Operations 433  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 434  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "MECHANISMS THAT REINFORCE CELL MEMORY IN PLANTS AND ANIMALS 435  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Patterns of DNA Methylation Can Be Inherited When Vertebrate Cells Divide 435  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CG-Rich Islands Are Associated with Many Genes in Mammals 436  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Genomic Imprinting Is Based on DNA Methylation 438  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Chromosome-wide Alteration in Chromatin Structure Can Be Inherited 440  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Mammalian X-Inactivation in Females Is Triggered by the Synthesis of a Long Noncoding RNA 442  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Stable Patterns of Gene Expression Can Be Transmitted to Daughter Cells 443  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 445  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "POST-TRANSCRIPTIONAL CONTROLS 445  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Attenuation Causes the Premature Termination of Some RNA Molecules 445  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Riboswitches Probably Represent Ancient Forms of Gene Control 446  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Alternative RNA Splicing Can Produce Different Forms of a Protein from the Same Gene 446  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Definition of a Gene Has Been Modified Since the Discovery of Alternative RNA Splicing 448  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Back Splicing Can Produce Circular RNA Molecules 449  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Change in the Site of RNA Transcript Cleavage and Poly-A Addition Can Change the C-terminus of a Protein 449  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Nucleotides in mRNA Can Be Covalently Modified 450  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Editing Can Change the Meaning of the RNA Message 451  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Human AIDS Virus Illustrates How RNA Transport from the Nucleus Can Be Regulated 452  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "mRNAs Can Be Localized to Specific Regions of the Cytosol 453  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Untranslated Regions of mRNAs Control Their Translation 456  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Phosphorylation of an Initiation Factor Regulates Protein Synthesis Globally 457  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Initiation at AUG Codons Upstream of the Translation Start Can Regulate Eukaryotic Translation Initiation 458  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Internal Ribosome Entry Sites Also Provide Opportunities for Translational Control 458  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Changes in mRNA Stability Can Control Gene Expression 459  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Regulation of mRNA Stability Involves P-bodies and Stress Granules 461  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 462  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "REGULATION OF GENE EXPRESSION BY NONCODING RNAs 462  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Small Noncoding RNA Transcripts Regulate Many Animal and Plant Genes Through RNA Interference 462  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "miRNAs Regulate mRNA Translation and Stability 463  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Interference Also Serves as a Cell Defense Mechanism 464  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Interference Can Direct Heterochromatin Formation 465  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "piRNAs Protect the Germ Line from Transposable Elements 466  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Interference Has Become a Powerful Experimental Tool 467  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Have Additional Mechanisms to Hold Transposons and Integrated Viral Genomes in Check 467  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacteria Use Small Noncoding RNAs to Protect Themselves from Viruses 468  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Long Noncoding RNAs Have Diverse Functions in the Cell 469  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 471  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 472  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 474"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.527,
            0.785,
            0.923,
            0.937
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chapter 8 Analyzing Cells, Molecules, and Systems 475  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "ISOLATING CELLS AND GROWING THEM IN CULTURE 476  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Can Be Isolated from Tissues and Grown in Culture 476  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Cell Lines Are a Widely Used Source of Homogeneous Cells 478  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hybridoma Cell Lines Are Factories That Produce Monoclonal Antibodies 478  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 480  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "PURIFYING PROTEINS 480  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Can Be Separated into Their Component Fractions 480  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cell Extracts Provide Accessible Systems to Study Cell Functions 482  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins Can Be Separated by Chromatography 483"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
